  <model metaid="meta_canagliflozin_intestine" id="canagliflozin_intestine" name="Model for canagliflozin absorption in the small intestine" substanceUnits="mmole" timeUnits="min" volumeUnits="litre" areaUnits="m2" lengthUnits="metre" extentUnits="mmole">
    <notes>
      <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
        <h1>Model for canagliflozin absorption</h1>
        <ul>
          <li>Dissolution of the oral dose and absorption of canagliflozin; the
fraction absorbed <code>F_can_abs</code> of the canagliflozin of the lumen is
absorbed, the rest is excreted in the feces. The absolute
bioavailability is 65 % (90 % CI 55-76 %) [Devineni2015b].</li>
          <li>The glucuronides M5 and M7 of the bile are hydrolysed to canagliflozin in
the lumen (enterohepatic circulation), the source of most of the
canagliflozin of the feces (39-41.5 % of the dose). M7 which is not
hydrolysed is excreted in the feces (2-3 % of the dose), M5 is not
found in the feces [Mamidi2014, Devineni2015b]. M9 of the bile is
excreted in the feces (7-8 %).</li>
          <li>The absorption of canagliflozin is not changed by food: a high fat
breakfast changes neither the AUC (+8 %) nor Cmax (+0.5 %)
[Devineni2015e], so the model has no food effect.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The content of this model has been carefully created in a manual research effort.</p>
